<UserControl x:Class="Barista.Views.ComponentsPage"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <StackPanel>
    <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource H1}" Text="What to install"/>
    <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Lede}">
      These are three independent things, not three steps of one thing.
    </TextBlock>

    <Border Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}">
      <StackPanel>
        <CheckBox x:Name="ChkProvider" IsChecked="True" Checked="OnChanged" Unchecked="OnChanged"
                  Margin="0,0,0,4">
          <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold" Text="The credential provider (the logon tile)"/>
        </CheckBox>
        <TextBlock Margin="22,0,0,12" Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}" MaxWidth="620">
          Adds a tile that asks for a password and a one-time code. On its own it is offered
          next to the usual tiles, not instead of them. Runs in LogonUI, not lsass — a bug here
          costs you a tile, and Safe Mode still gets you in. This is the low-risk half of the
          project.
        </TextBlock>

        <CheckBox x:Name="ChkProviderFilter" IsChecked="True" Checked="OnChanged"
                  Unchecked="OnChanged" Margin="22,0,0,4">
          <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold" Text="…and make it the only way in"/>
        </CheckBox>
        <TextBlock Margin="44,0,0,0" Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}" MaxWidth="600">
          Registers the provider filter, which hides the password, PIN and Hello tiles. Without
          this the second factor is optional in practice — anyone at the logon screen can pick
          the password tile and skip the code — so it is on by default. It is a separate stage
          with its own restart, run after enrollment and before the network changes, because a
          tile that does not work and no other tile is a console nobody can log in to.
        </TextBlock>
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>

    <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold" Margin="2,6,0,2" Text="Non-interactive logons (SMB, WinRM, scheduled tasks)"/>
    <TextBlock Margin="2,0,0,10" Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}" MaxWidth="640">
      The tile never sees these. Pick one — the two filters both want the single MSV1_0 Auth0
      slot, so they are genuinely exclusive.
    </TextBlock>

    <Border Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}">
      <StackPanel>
        <RadioButton x:Name="RbDeny" GroupName="Net" IsChecked="True"
                     Checked="OnChanged">
          <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold">
            Deny rights only <Run Foreground="{StaticResource Good}" FontWeight="Normal"
                                  Text="— recommended"/>
          </TextBlock>
        </RadioButton>
        <TextBlock Margin="22,2,0,14" Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}" MaxWidth="620">
          Sets the built-in "Deny log on" user rights for the account. Supported by Microsoft,
          survives reboots, runs no code in lsass and cannot stop the machine booting. If your
          goal is hardening rather than learning the layer, this is the whole answer and you can
          leave the other two alone.
        </TextBlock>

        <CheckBox x:Name="ChkKeepNetwork" Margin="40,0,0,14" Checked="OnChanged" Unchecked="OnChanged">
          <TextBlock MaxWidth="580" Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}">
            Keep the network right, so RDP with NLA still works. SMB and WinRM then still take
            the password alone for this account. There is no setting that gives you both.
          </TextBlock>
        </CheckBox>

        <RadioButton x:Name="RbDoppioFilter" GroupName="Net" Checked="OnChanged">
          <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold" Text="Doppio's sub-authentication filter — refuse network logons outright"/>
        </RadioButton>
        <TextBlock Margin="22,2,0,14" Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}" MaxWidth="620">
          Unsigned DLL in lsass. Refuses every network logon for an enrolled account, which also
          means that account can never map a share and RDP with NLA stops working for it.
        </TextBlock>

        <RadioButton x:Name="RbRistretto" GroupName="Net" Checked="OnChanged">
          <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold" Text="Ristretto — gate network logons behind a one-time code"/>
        </RadioButton>
        <TextBlock x:Name="RistrettoNote" Margin="22,2,0,0" Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}"
                   MaxWidth="620">
          Unsigned DLL in lsass. Same refusal, unless a gate was opened with a TOTP in the last
          few minutes. Note what it costs: while a gate is open, anyone who can authenticate as
          that account gets in, not only you. And you have to be at this machine, elevated, to
          open one.
        </TextBlock>
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>

    <Border Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}">
      <StackPanel>
        <CheckBox x:Name="ChkAuthPackage" Checked="OnChanged" Unchecked="OnChanged" Margin="0,0,0,4">
          <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold" Text="The authentication package (closes nothing)"/>
        </CheckBox>
        <TextBlock Margin="22,0,0,0" Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}" MaxWidth="620">
          Only ever called by a logon that addresses it by package id, which no normal logon
          does. It is in the project to show how the layer works. Install it to watch it load,
          not because it protects anything.
        </TextBlock>
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
